<!-- 5 ▸ BELIEF 1+2 · IDENTIFICATION / "IT'S NOT YOU" -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="center"><span class="eyebrow">If you have tried the lot</span></div>
    <h2>You've tried everything, <em>and nothing lasted.</em><br>That's not on you.</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p><strong>The wheat bag helps for ten minutes.</strong> Then it goes cold and you're back at the microwave for the fourth time tonight.</p>
        <p><strong>The pills work until they wear off.</strong> You'd rather not live on them anyway.</p>
        <p><strong>The physio helps for a few days.</strong> Then the stiffness comes back, £50 a visit, next appointment three weeks off.</p>
        <p>None of them failed because your joints are "too far gone." They gave you a <strong>moment</strong> of ease. A stiff knee or shoulder needs warmth it can count on, at the same time every day.</p>
        
        <blockquote class="pull">Warmth that lasts is the part the cupboard cannot do. Everything in there gives you roughly the right amount of heat, then goes cold and asks you to sit still for all of it.
          <footer>Why we built it this way</footer>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="{{ 'th-in-use.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="A woman relaxing on a sofa wearing the Jointwell wrap on her knee, cordless and Type-C rechargeable" width="1000" height="1000" loading="lazy">
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 6 ▸ BELIEF 2 · THE STIFFNESS LOOP -->
<!-- 5c ▸ IDENTIFICATION ACROSS THE AGE RANGE -->

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="center"><span class="eyebrow">Two very different people read this page</span></div>
    <h2>Thirty-eight or seventy-eight, <em>the mornings are the same.</em></h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p><strong>If you are in your thirties or forties,</strong> you have probably been told you are too young for this. Possibly by someone holding a clipboard. So you got on with it, because saying it out loud at thirty-eight feels like admitting something.</p>
        <p>You stopped running, or stopped kneeling to do the job properly, or started thinking about which side of the car park you leave the car on. You take the lift when there is one and you make it look like a choice. Nobody at work knows, and you would rather it stayed that way.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>If you are in your sixties or seventies,</strong> you have had the other version. Wear and tear. What do you expect at your age. Take some paracetamol and come back if it gets worse.</p>
        <p>Opposite sentence, same ending. Both of you were told there was nothing worth doing about it yet, and both of you stopped bringing it up.</p>
        <p>A cold joint does not ask how old you are. Neither does half an hour of warmth in the morning.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="center"><span class="eyebrow">Why relief never lasted</span></div>
    <h2>Stiff joints get stuck in <em>a loop.</em></h2>
    <p class="lede">Knees, shoulders and elbows all sit close to the surface with little muscle around them to hold warmth. When one hurts, a cycle starts, and a quick fix pauses it for an hour.</p>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="loopbox">
        <ol class="loop" role="list">
          <li><span class="n">1</span><div><b>The joint hurts</b><span>so you use it less than you used to</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="n">2</span><div><b>You move less</b><span>and the less it moves, the less warmth it holds</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="n">3</span><div><b>It cools and tightens</b><span>a cold joint stiffens fastest overnight</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="n">4</span><div><b>Stiffness hurts more</b><span>which makes you move it even less</span></div></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="loop-foot"><span aria-hidden="true">↻</span> Then round it goes again, every morning.</p>
        <p class="loop-note">Why short bursts of warmth don't hold. This is background, not a medical diagnosis.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-weight:800;font-size:24px;margin-bottom:12px">Pain, less movement, less warmth, more pain.</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);margin-bottom:12px">A bad knee or shoulder is worst in the morning, worse in winter, and locks up after an hour in a chair. Sit still and it cools; cool and it feels tighter still.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft);margin-bottom:12px">A ten-minute heat pack is warm, then it isn't, and you are back where you started. What the cupboard cannot give you is warmth that is <strong style="color:var(--ink)">even, and there tomorrow, and there the day after</strong>. Nothing in it holds heat that long.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-soft)">You already know heat helps. You've known it for years, which is why you own the wheat bag. Keeping the heat is the hard part.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

